```python
class Collection:
    def add(self, item):
        self._items.append(item)
    def add_all(self, items):
        for i in items: self.add(i)  # calls self.add — internal detail

class CountingCollection(Collection):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__(); self.count = 0
    def add(self, item):
        self.count += 1
        super().add(item)
```
`CountingCollection` relies on `add_all` calling `self.add` internally — nowhere documented, just observed. If `Collection.add_all` is later "optimized" to append directly without calling `add`, `CountingCollection.count` silently goes wrong, with no error, no test failure the base class's author would think to run.

```python
class Collection:
    def add(self, item):
        self._items.append(item)
        self._on_add(item)  # documented extension hook, contract now explicit
    def _on_add(self, item):
        pass  # subclasses override this, not add() itself

class CountingCollection(Collection):
    def _on_add(self, item):
        self.count += 1
```
`add_all`'s call to `add` — and `add`'s call to `_on_add` — is now a documented, frozen contract, not an accident a subclass happened to depend on. `CountingCollection` no longer overrides `add` at all, so it can't be broken by a future "optimization" to `add_all`.

